# Custom C++ and CUDA Operators — Complete Content Extraction

**Source:** PyTorch Tutorials 2.14.0+cu130 documentation
**Author:** [Richard Zou](https://github.com/zou3519)
**Dates:** Created Jun 18, 2024 | Last Updated Jan 20, 2026 | Last Verified Nov 05, 2024

---

## Overview

**What you will learn:**
- How to integrate custom operators written in C++/CUDA with PyTorch
- How to test custom operators using `torch.library.opcheck`

**Prerequisites:**
- **PyTorch 2.4 or later** (or **PyTorch 2.10 or later if using the stable ABI**)
- Basic understanding of C++ and CUDA programming

> **Note:** This tutorial will also work on AMD ROCm with no additional modifications.

PyTorch offers a large library of operators that work on Tensors (e.g. `torch.add`, `torch.sum`, etc). This tutorial demonstrates the blessed path to authoring a custom operator written in C++/CUDA.

The tutorial authors a **fused multiply-add** C++ and CUDA operator with these semantics:

```python
def mymuladd(a: Tensor, b: Tensor, c: float):
    return a * b + c
```

The end-to-end working example is in the [extension-cpp](https://github.com/pytorch/extension-cpp) repository, containing two parallel implementations:
- [extension_cpp/](https://github.com/pytorch/extension-cpp/tree/master/extension_cpp): Uses the standard ATen/LibTorch API.
- [extension_cpp_stable/](https://github.com/pytorch/extension-cpp/tree/master/extension_cpp_stable): Uses APIs supported by the LibTorch Stable ABI (recommended for PyTorch 2.10+).

**Which API should you use?**
- **ABI-Stable LibTorch API** (recommended): For PyTorch 2.10+. Build a **single wheel** that works across multiple PyTorch versions (2.10, 2.11, 2.12, etc.), reducing maintenance burden.
- **Non-ABI-Stable LibTorch API**: Use if you need APIs not yet available in the stable ABI, or if targeting PyTorch versions older than 2.10. You will need to build separate wheels for each PyTorch version.

---

## 1. Setting Up the Build System

Use `torch.utils.cpp_extension` to compile custom C++/CUDA code. C++ extensions may be built "ahead of time" with setuptools, or "just in time" via [load_inline](https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/cpp_extension.html#torch.utils.cpp_extension.load_inline); the tutorial focuses on the "ahead of time" flavor.

### `setup.py` — ABI-Stable LibTorch API

```python
from setuptools import setup, Extension
from torch.utils import cpp_extension

setup(name="extension_cpp",
      ext_modules=[
          cpp_extension.CppExtension(
              "extension_cpp",
              ["muladd.cpp"],
              extra_compile_args={
                  "cxx": [
                      # define Py_LIMITED_API with min version 3.9 to expose only the stable
                      # limited API subset from Python.h
                      "-DPy_LIMITED_API=0x03090000",
                      # define TORCH_TARGET_VERSION with min version 2.10 to expose only the
                      # stable API subset from torch
                      "-DTORCH_TARGET_VERSION=0x020a000000000000",
                  ]
              },
              py_limited_api=True)],  # Build 1 wheel across multiple Python versions
      cmdclass={'build_ext': cpp_extension.BuildExtension},
      options={"bdist_wheel": {"py_limited_api": "cp39"}}  # 3.9 is minimum supported Python version
)
```

### `setup.py` — Non-ABI-Stable LibTorch API

```python
from setuptools import setup, Extension
from torch.utils import cpp_extension

setup(name="extension_cpp",
      ext_modules=[
          cpp_extension.CppExtension(
              "extension_cpp",
              ["muladd.cpp"],
              extra_compile_args={
                  "cxx": [
                      "-DPy_LIMITED_API=0x03090000",
                  ]
              },
              py_limited_api=True)],
      cmdclass={'build_ext': cpp_extension.BuildExtension},
      options={"bdist_wheel": {"py_limited_api": "cp39"}}
)
```

> If you need to compile CUDA code (for example, `.cu` files), then instead use **`torch.utils.cpp_extension.CUDAExtension`**. See [extension-cpp](https://github.com/pytorch/extension-cpp) for an example of how this is set up.

### CPython Agnosticism

The above examples represent a **CPython agnostic wheel** — a single wheel that can run across multiple CPython versions. The minimum supported version is 3.9. Build the extension in the same environment as the minimum CPython version (here, CPython 3.9).

Three key lines to note:

1. **`Py_LIMITED_API`** in `extra_compile_args`:
```python
extra_compile_args={"cxx": ["-DPy_LIMITED_API=0x03090000"]},
```
Defining `Py_LIMITED_API` verifies the extension only uses the [CPython Stable Limited API](https://docs.python.org/3/c-api/stable.html), a requirement for a CPython agnostic wheel. Avoid unstable CPython APIs (e.g., from `libtorch_python`, in particular pytorch/python bindings) and only use APIs from `libtorch` (ATen objects, operators and the dispatcher). Defining this flag is not a full guarantee but is strongly recommended.

2. & 3. **`py_limited_api`** (informs setuptools and influences wheel naming):
```python
setup(name="extension_cpp",
      ext_modules=[
          cpp_extension.CppExtension(
            ...,
            py_limited_api=True)],  # Build 1 wheel across multiple Python versions
      ...,
      options={"bdist_wheel": {"py_limited_api": "cp39"}}  # 3.9 is minimum supported Python version
)
```
Both placements are necessary (`py_limited_api=True` as an argument to `CppExtension`/`CUDAExtension`, and as an option to the `"bdist_wheel"` command with the minimal supported CPython version).

If your extension uses CPython APIs outside the stable limited set, you **cannot** build a CPython agnostic wheel — build one wheel per CPython version:

```python
from setuptools import setup, Extension
from torch.utils import cpp_extension

setup(name="extension_cpp",
      ext_modules=[
          cpp_extension.CppExtension(
            "extension_cpp",
            ["muladd.cpp"])],
      cmdclass={'build_ext': cpp_extension.BuildExtension},
)
```

### LibTorch Stable ABI (PyTorch Agnosticism)

LibTorch agnosticism allows building a single wheel that works across multiple PyTorch versions (2.10, 2.11, 2.12, etc.). CPython agnosticism and LibTorch agnosticism are orthogonal and can be combined.

To achieve LibTorch agnosticism, use the ABI stable LibTorch API — e.g., instead of `at::Tensor`, use `torch::stable::Tensor`. See the [LibTorch Stable ABI documentation](https://pytorch.org/docs/main/notes/libtorch_stable_abi.html).

The stable ABI `setup.py` includes `TORCH_TARGET_VERSION=0x020a000000000000`. **Version format: `[MAJ 1 byte][MIN 1 byte][PATCH 1 byte][ABI TAG 5 bytes]`, so 2.10.0 = `0x020a000000000000`.**

---

## 2. Defining the Custom Op and Adding Backend Implementations

### CPU implementation — ABI-Stable LibTorch API

```cpp
#include <torch/csrc/stable/library.h>
#include <torch/csrc/stable/ops.h>
#include <torch/csrc/stable/tensor.h>
#include <torch/headeronly/core/ScalarType.h>
#include <torch/headeronly/macros/Macros.h>

torch::stable::Tensor mymuladd_cpu(
    const torch::stable::Tensor& a,
    const torch::stable::Tensor& b,
    double c) {
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.sizes().equals(b.sizes()));
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.scalar_type() == torch::headeronly::ScalarType::Float);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(b.scalar_type() == torch::headeronly::ScalarType::Float);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.device().type() == torch::headeronly::DeviceType::CPU);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(b.device().type() == torch::headeronly::DeviceType::CPU);

  torch::stable::Tensor a_contig = torch::stable::contiguous(a);
  torch::stable::Tensor b_contig = torch::stable::contiguous(b);
  torch::stable::Tensor result = torch::stable::empty_like(a_contig);

  const float* a_ptr = a_contig.const_data_ptr<float>();
  const float* b_ptr = b_contig.const_data_ptr<float>();
  float* result_ptr = result.mutable_data_ptr<float>();

  for (int64_t i = 0; i < result.numel(); i++) {
    result_ptr[i] = a_ptr[i] * b_ptr[i] + c;
  }
  return result;
}
```

### CPU implementation — Non-ABI-Stable LibTorch API

```cpp
#include <ATen/Operators.h>
#include <torch/all.h>
#include <torch/library.h>

at::Tensor mymuladd_cpu(const at::Tensor& a, const at::Tensor& b, double c) {
  TORCH_CHECK(a.sizes() == b.sizes());
  TORCH_CHECK(a.dtype() == at::kFloat);
  TORCH_CHECK(b.dtype() == at::kFloat);
  TORCH_INTERNAL_ASSERT(a.device().type() == at::DeviceType::CPU);
  TORCH_INTERNAL_ASSERT(b.device().type() == at::DeviceType::CPU);
  at::Tensor a_contig = a.contiguous();
  at::Tensor b_contig = b.contiguous();
  at::Tensor result = torch::empty(a_contig.sizes(), a_contig.options());
  const float* a_ptr = a_contig.data_ptr<float>();
  const float* b_ptr = b_contig.data_ptr<float>();
  float* result_ptr = result.data_ptr<float>();
  for (int64_t i = 0; i < result.numel(); i++) {
    result_ptr[i] = a_ptr[i] * b_ptr[i] + c;
  }
  return result;
}
```

To use this from PyTorch's Python frontend, register it as a PyTorch operator using the `TORCH_LIBRARY` (or `STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY`) macro. This automatically binds the operator to Python.

**Operator registration is a two-step process:**
1. **Defining the operator** — ensures PyTorch is aware of the new operator.
2. **Registering backend implementations** — associates implementations for various backends (CPU, CUDA) with the operator.

### Defining an Operator

Steps:
1. Select a namespace (recommended: your top-level project name; here "extension_cpp").
2. Provide a schema string specifying input/output types and whether any input Tensor will be mutated. (More types than Tensor and float are supported — see [The Custom Operators Manual](https://pytorch.org/docs/main/notes/custom_operators.html).)
   - If authoring an operator that can mutate its input Tensors, see the "Creating mutable operators" section.

**ABI-Stable LibTorch API:**
```cpp
STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY(extension_cpp, m) {
  // Note that "float" in the schema corresponds to the C++ double type
  // and the Python float type.
  m.def("mymuladd(Tensor a, Tensor b, float c) -> Tensor");
}
```

**Non-ABI-Stable LibTorch API:**
```cpp
TORCH_LIBRARY(extension_cpp, m) {
  // Note that "float" in the schema corresponds to the C++ double type
  // and the Python float type.
  m.def("mymuladd(Tensor a, Tensor b, float c) -> Tensor");
}
```

This makes the operator available from Python via `torch.ops.extension_cpp.mymuladd`.

### Registering Backend Implementations

Use `TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL` (or `STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL`) to register a backend implementation.

**ABI-Stable LibTorch API** — note the function pointer is wrapped with `TORCH_BOX()` (required for stable ABI functions to handle argument boxing/unboxing correctly):
```cpp
STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL(extension_cpp, CPU, m) {
  m.impl("mymuladd", TORCH_BOX(&mymuladd_cpu));
}
```

**Non-ABI-Stable LibTorch API:**
```cpp
TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL(extension_cpp, CPU, m) {
  m.impl("mymuladd", &mymuladd_cpu);
}
```

### CUDA Kernel + Registration — ABI-Stable LibTorch API

```cpp
#include <torch/csrc/stable/library.h>
#include <torch/csrc/stable/ops.h>
#include <torch/csrc/stable/tensor.h>
#include <torch/csrc/stable/c/shim.h>
#include <cuda.h>
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__global__ void muladd_kernel(int numel, const float* a, const float* b, float c, float* result) {
  int idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
  if (idx < numel) result[idx] = a[idx] * b[idx] + c;
}

torch::stable::Tensor mymuladd_cuda(
    const torch::stable::Tensor& a,
    const torch::stable::Tensor& b,
    double c) {
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.sizes().equals(b.sizes()));
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.scalar_type() == torch::headeronly::ScalarType::Float);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(b.scalar_type() == torch::headeronly::ScalarType::Float);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.device().type() == torch::headeronly::DeviceType::CUDA);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(b.device().type() == torch::headeronly::DeviceType::CUDA);

  torch::stable::Tensor a_contig = torch::stable::contiguous(a);
  torch::stable::Tensor b_contig = torch::stable::contiguous(b);
  torch::stable::Tensor result = torch::stable::empty_like(a_contig);

  const float* a_ptr = a_contig.const_data_ptr<float>();
  const float* b_ptr = b_contig.const_data_ptr<float>();
  float* result_ptr = result.mutable_data_ptr<float>();

  int numel = a_contig.numel();

  // For now, we rely on the raw shim API to get the current CUDA stream.
  // This will be improved in a future release.
  // When using a raw shim API, we need to use TORCH_ERROR_CODE_CHECK to
  // check the error code and throw an appropriate runtime_error otherwise.
  void* stream_ptr = nullptr;
  TORCH_ERROR_CODE_CHECK(
      aoti_torch_get_current_cuda_stream(a.get_device_index(), &stream_ptr));
  cudaStream_t stream = static_cast<cudaStream_t>(stream_ptr);

  muladd_kernel<<<(numel+255)/256, 256, 0, stream>>>(numel, a_ptr, b_ptr, c, result_ptr);
  return result;
}

STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL(extension_cpp, CUDA, m) {
  m.impl("mymuladd", TORCH_BOX(&mymuladd_cuda));
}
```

### CUDA Kernel + Registration — Non-ABI-Stable LibTorch API

```cpp
#include <ATen/Operators.h>
#include <torch/all.h>
#include <torch/library.h>
#include <cuda.h>
#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <ATen/cuda/CUDAContext.h>

__global__ void muladd_kernel(int numel, const float* a, const float* b, float c, float* result) {
  int idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
  if (idx < numel) result[idx] = a[idx] * b[idx] + c;
}

at::Tensor mymuladd_cuda(const at::Tensor& a, const at::Tensor& b, double c) {
  TORCH_CHECK(a.sizes() == b.sizes());
  TORCH_CHECK(a.dtype() == at::kFloat);
  TORCH_CHECK(b.dtype() == at::kFloat);
  TORCH_INTERNAL_ASSERT(a.device().type() == at::DeviceType::CUDA);
  TORCH_INTERNAL_ASSERT(b.device().type() == at::DeviceType::CUDA);
  at::Tensor a_contig = a.contiguous();
  at::Tensor b_contig = b.contiguous();
  at::Tensor result = at::empty(a_contig.sizes(), a_contig.options());
  const float* a_ptr = a_contig.data_ptr<float>();
  const float* b_ptr = b_contig.data_ptr<float>();
  float* result_ptr = result.data_ptr<float>();

  int numel = a_contig.numel();
  cudaStream_t stream = at::cuda::getCurrentCUDAStream();
  muladd_kernel<<<(numel+255)/256, 256, 0, stream>>>(numel, a_ptr, b_ptr, c, result_ptr);
  return result;
}

TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL(extension_cpp, CUDA, m) {
  m.impl("mymuladd", &mymuladd_cuda);
}
```

---

## 3. Adding `torch.compile` Support (FakeTensor / Meta Registration)

To add `torch.compile` support, add a **FakeTensor kernel** (also known as a "meta kernel" or "abstract impl"). FakeTensors have metadata (shape, dtype, device) but no data — the FakeTensor kernel specifies how to compute output metadata from input metadata. It should return dummy Tensors with the correct metadata (shape/strides/`dtype`/device).

Recommended from Python via **`torch.library.register_fake`** (though doable from C++):

```python
# Important: the C++ custom operator definitions should be loaded first
# before calling ``torch.library`` APIs that add registrations for the
# C++ custom operator(s). The following import loads our
# C++ custom operator definitions.
# Note that if you are striving for Python agnosticism, you should use
# the ``load_library(...)`` API call instead. See the next section for
# more details.
from . import _C

@torch.library.register_fake("extension_cpp::mymuladd")
def _(a, b, c):
    torch._check(a.shape == b.shape)
    torch._check(a.dtype == torch.float)
    torch._check(b.dtype == torch.float)
    torch._check(a.device == b.device)
    return torch.empty_like(a)
```

---

## 4. Setting Up Hybrid Python/C++ Registration

The tutorial defines the custom operator in C++, adds CPU/CUDA implementations in C++, and adds `FakeTensor` kernels and backward formulas in Python. **Registration order matters** (importing in the wrong order leads to an error). Load the C++ library holding the operator definition first, then call the `torch.library` registration APIs. Three ways:

**Way 1** — Define a dummy Python module for `_C` using `PYBIND11_MODULE` (does NOT compile with `Py_LIMITED_API` because pybind11 does not promise to only use the stable limited CPython API, so you cannot build a CPython agnostic wheel):

```cpp
// in, say, not_agnostic/csrc/extension_BAD.cpp
#include <pybind11/pybind11.h>

PYBIND11_MODULE("_C", m) {}
```
```python
# in, say, extension/__init__.py
from . import _C
```

**Way 2** (used in this tutorial) — Replace the unstable `PYBIND11` call with stable API calls. Compiles with `-DPy_LIMITED_API=0x03090000` and creates a dummy Python `_C` module. See [extension_cpp/__init__.py](https://github.com/pytorch/extension-cpp/blob/38ec45e/extension_cpp/__init__.py) and [extension_cpp/csrc/muladd.cpp](https://github.com/pytorch/extension-cpp/blob/38ec45e/extension_cpp/csrc/muladd.cpp):

```cpp
#include <Python.h>

extern "C" {
  /* Creates a dummy empty _C module that can be imported from Python.
    The import from Python will load the .so consisting of this file
    in this extension, so that the TORCH_LIBRARY static initializers
    below are run. */
  PyObject* PyInit__C(void)
  {
      static struct PyModuleDef module_def = {
          PyModuleDef_HEAD_INIT,
          "_C",   /* name of module */
          NULL,   /* module documentation, may be NULL */
          -1,     /* size of per-interpreter state of the module,
                    or -1 if the module keeps state in global variables. */
          NULL,   /* methods */
      };
      return PyModule_Create(&module_def);
  }
}
```
```python
from . import _C
```

**Way 3** — Avoid `Python.h` entirely by using `torch.ops.load_library("/path/to/library.so")` in Python to load the `.so` file(s). No `_C` Python module is created, so you cannot call `import _C`. The challenge is locating the `.so` files:

```python
import torch
from pathlib import Path

so_files = list(Path(__file__).parent.glob("_C*.so"))
assert (
    len(so_files) == 1
), f"Expected one _C*.so file, found {len(so_files)}"
torch.ops.load_library(so_files[0])

from . import ops
```

---

## 5. Adding Training (Autograd) Support

Use **`torch.library.register_autograd`** to add training support. Prefer this over directly using Python `torch.autograd.Function` (see [The Custom Operators Manual](https://pytorch.org/docs/main/notes/custom_operators.html)).

```python
def _backward(ctx, grad):
    a, b = ctx.saved_tensors
    grad_a, grad_b = None, None
    if ctx.needs_input_grad[0]:
        grad_a = grad * b
    if ctx.needs_input_grad[1]:
        grad_b = grad * a
    return grad_a, grad_b, None

def _setup_context(ctx, inputs, output):
    a, b, c = inputs
    saved_a, saved_b = None, None
    if ctx.needs_input_grad[0]:
        saved_b = b
    if ctx.needs_input_grad[1]:
        saved_a = a
    ctx.save_for_backward(saved_a, saved_b)

# This code adds training support for the operator. You must provide us
# the backward formula for the operator and a `setup_context` function
# to save values to be used in the backward.
torch.library.register_autograd(
    "extension_cpp::mymuladd", _backward, setup_context=_setup_context)
```

> **Note:** The backward must be a composition of PyTorch-understood operators. If you wish to use another custom C++ or CUDA kernel in your backward pass, it must be wrapped into a custom operator.

### Example: wrapping a custom `mymul` kernel and calling it from the backward

**ABI-Stable LibTorch API:**
```cpp
torch::stable::Tensor mymul_cpu(
    const torch::stable::Tensor& a,
    const torch::stable::Tensor& b) {
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.sizes().equals(b.sizes()));
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.scalar_type() == torch::headeronly::ScalarType::Float);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(b.scalar_type() == torch::headeronly::ScalarType::Float);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.device().type() == torch::headeronly::DeviceType::CPU);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(b.device().type() == torch::headeronly::DeviceType::CPU);

  torch::stable::Tensor a_contig = torch::stable::contiguous(a);
  torch::stable::Tensor b_contig = torch::stable::contiguous(b);
  torch::stable::Tensor result = torch::stable::empty_like(a_contig);

  const float* a_ptr = a_contig.const_data_ptr<float>();
  const float* b_ptr = b_contig.const_data_ptr<float>();
  float* result_ptr = result.mutable_data_ptr<float>();

  for (int64_t i = 0; i < result.numel(); i++) {
    result_ptr[i] = a_ptr[i] * b_ptr[i];
  }
  return result;
}

STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY(extension_cpp, m) {
  m.def("mymuladd(Tensor a, Tensor b, float c) -> Tensor");
  m.def("mymul(Tensor a, Tensor b) -> Tensor");
}

STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL(extension_cpp, CPU, m) {
  m.impl("mymuladd", TORCH_BOX(&mymuladd_cpu));
  m.impl("mymul", TORCH_BOX(&mymul_cpu));
}
```

**Non-ABI-Stable LibTorch API:**
```cpp
at::Tensor mymul_cpu(const at::Tensor& a, const at::Tensor& b) {
  TORCH_CHECK(a.sizes() == b.sizes());
  TORCH_CHECK(a.dtype() == at::kFloat);
  TORCH_CHECK(b.dtype() == at::kFloat);
  TORCH_INTERNAL_ASSERT(a.device().type() == at::DeviceType::CPU);
  TORCH_INTERNAL_ASSERT(b.device().type() == at::DeviceType::CPU);
  at::Tensor a_contig = a.contiguous();
  at::Tensor b_contig = b.contiguous();
  at::Tensor result = torch::empty(a_contig.sizes(), a_contig.options());
  const float* a_ptr = a_contig.data_ptr<float>();
  const float* b_ptr = b_contig.data_ptr<float>();
  float* result_ptr = result.data_ptr<float>();
  for (int64_t i = 0; i < result.numel(); i++) {
    result_ptr[i] = a_ptr[i] * b_ptr[i];
  }
  return result;
}

TORCH_LIBRARY(extension_cpp, m) {
  m.def("mymuladd(Tensor a, Tensor b, float c) -> Tensor");
  m.def("mymul(Tensor a, Tensor b) -> Tensor");
}

TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL(extension_cpp, CPU, m) {
  m.impl("mymuladd", &mymuladd_cpu);
  m.impl("mymul", &mymul_cpu);
}
```

**Python backward calling the custom operator:**
```python
def _backward(ctx, grad):
    a, b = ctx.saved_tensors
    grad_a, grad_b = None, None
    if ctx.needs_input_grad[0]:
        grad_a = torch.ops.extension_cpp.mymul.default(grad, b)
    if ctx.needs_input_grad[1]:
        grad_b = torch.ops.extension_cpp.mymul.default(grad, a)
    return grad_a, grad_b, None


def _setup_context(ctx, inputs, output):
    a, b, c = inputs
    saved_a, saved_b = None, None
    if ctx.needs_input_grad[0]:
        saved_b = b
    if ctx.needs_input_grad[1]:
        saved_a = a
    ctx.save_for_backward(saved_a, saved_b)


torch.library.register_autograd(
    "extension_cpp::mymuladd", _backward, setup_context=_setup_context)
```

---

## 6. Testing an Operator

Use **`torch.library.opcheck`** to test that the custom op was registered correctly. Note that this function does not test that the gradients are mathematically correct — plan to write separate tests for that, either manual ones or by using `torch.autograd.gradcheck`.

```python
def sample_inputs(device, *, requires_grad=False):
    def make_tensor(*size):
        return torch.randn(size, device=device, requires_grad=requires_grad)

    def make_nondiff_tensor(*size):
        return torch.randn(size, device=device, requires_grad=False)

    return [
        [make_tensor(3), make_tensor(3), 1],
        [make_tensor(20), make_tensor(20), 3.14],
        [make_tensor(20), make_nondiff_tensor(20), -123],
        [make_nondiff_tensor(2, 3), make_tensor(2, 3), -0.3],
    ]

def reference_muladd(a, b, c):
    return a * b + c

samples = sample_inputs(device, requires_grad=True)
samples.extend(sample_inputs(device, requires_grad=False))
for args in samples:
    # Correctness test
    result = torch.ops.extension_cpp.mymuladd(*args)
    expected = reference_muladd(*args)
    torch.testing.assert_close(result, expected)

    # Use opcheck to check for incorrect usage of operator registration APIs
    torch.library.opcheck(torch.ops.extension_cpp.mymuladd.default, args)
```

---

## 7. Creating Mutable Operators

Use `Tensor(a!)` to specify each mutable Tensor in the schema; otherwise there will be undefined behavior. If there are multiple mutated Tensors, use different names (e.g., `Tensor(a!)`, `Tensor(b!)`, `Tensor(c!)`).

Example: a `myadd_out(a, b, out)` operator, which writes the contents of `a+b` into `out`.

**ABI-Stable LibTorch API:**
```cpp
void myadd_out_cpu(
    const torch::stable::Tensor& a,
    const torch::stable::Tensor& b,
    torch::stable::Tensor& out) {
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.sizes().equals(b.sizes()));
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(b.sizes().equals(out.sizes()));
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.scalar_type() == torch::headeronly::ScalarType::Float);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(b.scalar_type() == torch::headeronly::ScalarType::Float);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(out.scalar_type() == torch::headeronly::ScalarType::Float);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(out.is_contiguous());
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.device().type() == torch::headeronly::DeviceType::CPU);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(b.device().type() == torch::headeronly::DeviceType::CPU);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(out.device().type() == torch::headeronly::DeviceType::CPU);

  torch::stable::Tensor a_contig = torch::stable::contiguous(a);
  torch::stable::Tensor b_contig = torch::stable::contiguous(b);

  const float* a_ptr = a_contig.const_data_ptr<float>();
  const float* b_ptr = b_contig.const_data_ptr<float>();
  float* result_ptr = out.mutable_data_ptr<float>();

  for (int64_t i = 0; i < out.numel(); i++) {
    result_ptr[i] = a_ptr[i] + b_ptr[i];
  }
}
```

**Operator definition (schema specifying mutation) — ABI-Stable:**
```cpp
STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY(extension_cpp, m) {
  m.def("mymuladd(Tensor a, Tensor b, float c) -> Tensor");
  m.def("mymul(Tensor a, Tensor b) -> Tensor");
  m.def("myadd_out(Tensor a, Tensor b, Tensor(a!) out) -> ()");
}

STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL(extension_cpp, CPU, m) {
  m.impl("mymuladd", TORCH_BOX(&mymuladd_cpu));
  m.impl("mymul", TORCH_BOX(&mymul_cpu));
  m.impl("myadd_out", TORCH_BOX(&myadd_out_cpu));
}
```

**Non-ABI-Stable LibTorch API:**
```cpp
void myadd_out_cpu(const at::Tensor& a, const at::Tensor& b, at::Tensor& out) {
  TORCH_CHECK(a.sizes() == b.sizes());
  TORCH_CHECK(b.sizes() == out.sizes());
  TORCH_CHECK(a.dtype() == at::kFloat);
  TORCH_CHECK(b.dtype() == at::kFloat);
  TORCH_CHECK(out.dtype() == at::kFloat);
  TORCH_CHECK(out.is_contiguous());
  TORCH_INTERNAL_ASSERT(a.device().type() == at::DeviceType::CPU);
  TORCH_INTERNAL_ASSERT(b.device().type() == at::DeviceType::CPU);
  TORCH_INTERNAL_ASSERT(out.device().type() == at::DeviceType::CPU);
  at::Tensor a_contig = a.contiguous();
  at::Tensor b_contig = b.contiguous();
  const float* a_ptr = a_contig.data_ptr<float>();
  const float* b_ptr = b_contig.data_ptr<float>();
  float* result_ptr = out.data_ptr<float>();
  for (int64_t i = 0; i < out.numel(); i++) {
    result_ptr[i] = a_ptr[i] + b_ptr[i];
  }
}
```

**Operator definition (schema specifying mutation) — Non-ABI-Stable:**
```cpp
TORCH_LIBRARY(extension_cpp, m) {
  m.def("mymuladd(Tensor a, Tensor b, float c) -> Tensor");
  m.def("mymul(Tensor a, Tensor b) -> Tensor");
  m.def("myadd_out(Tensor a, Tensor b, Tensor(a!) out) -> ()");
}

TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL(extension_cpp, CPU, m) {
  m.impl("mymuladd", &mymuladd_cpu);
  m.impl("mymul", &mymul_cpu);
  m.impl("myadd_out", &myadd_out_cpu);
}
```

> **Note:** Do not return any mutated Tensors as outputs of the operator as this will cause incompatibility with PyTorch subsystems like `torch.compile`.

---

## 8. Conclusion

The tutorial covered the recommended approach to integrating Custom C++ and CUDA operators with PyTorch. The `TORCH_LIBRARY`/`STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY` and `torch.library` APIs are fairly low-level. For more information, see [The Custom Operators Manual](https://pytorch.org/tutorials/advanced/custom_ops_landing_page.html#the-custom-operators-manual).

---

## Quick Reference: Key API Names, Macros & Parameters

| Category | API / Macro / Parameter |
|---|---|
| **Build** | `torch.utils.cpp_extension.CppExtension`, `torch.utils.cpp_extension.CUDAExtension`, `torch.utils.cpp_extension.load_inline`, `cpp_extension.BuildExtension` |
| **Compile flags** | `-DPy_LIMITED_API=0x03090000`, `-DTORCH_TARGET_VERSION=0x020a000000000000` |
| **setuptools options** | `py_limited_api=True`, `options={"bdist_wheel": {"py_limited_api": "cp39"}}` |
| **Op definition** | `TORCH_LIBRARY(ns, m)`, `STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY(ns, m)`, `m.def("...")` |
| **Backend impl** | `TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL(ns, CPU/CUDA, m)`, `STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL(ns, CPU/CUDA, m)`, `m.impl("name", ...)` |
| **Stable ABI boxing** | `TORCH_BOX(&fn)` |
| **Stable ABI types** | `torch::stable::Tensor`, `torch::headeronly::ScalarType::Float`, `torch::headeronly::DeviceType::CPU/CUDA` |
| **Stable checks** | `STD_TORCH_CHECK(...)` vs `TORCH_CHECK(...)` |
| **Stable ops** | `torch::stable::contiguous`, `torch::stable::empty_like`, `const_data_ptr<float>()`, `mutable_data_ptr<float>()` |
| **CUDA stream (stable)** | `aoti_torch_get_current_cuda_stream`, `TORCH_ERROR_CODE_CHECK(...)` |
| **CUDA stream (non-stable)** | `at::cuda::getCurrentCUDAStream()` |
| **Fake/meta** | `torch.library.register_fake("ns::op")` |
| **Autograd** | `torch.library.register_autograd("ns::op", _backward, setup_context=_setup_context)` |
| **Library loading** | `from . import _C`, `PyInit__C`, `torch.ops.load_library("/path/to/library.so")` |
| **Testing** | `torch.library.opcheck(op, args)`, `torch.testing.assert_close`, `torch.autograd.gradcheck` |
| **Mutable schema** | `Tensor(a!)`, `Tensor(a!)`, `Tensor(b!)`, `Tensor(c!)` |
| **Version format** | `[MAJ 1 byte][MIN 1 byte][PATCH 1 byte][ABI TAG 5 bytes]` → 2.10.0 = `0x020a000000000000` |

**Required PyTorch version:** PyTorch **2.4 or later** (or PyTorch **2.10 or later** if using the stable ABI).

**Additional resources:** [The Custom Operators Manual](https://pytorch.org/docs/main/notes/custom_operators.html), [Custom Python Operators](https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/advanced/python_custom_ops.html#python-custom-ops-tutorial), [LibTorch Stable ABI docs](https://pytorch.org/docs/main/notes/libtorch_stable_abi.html), [extension-cpp repo](https://github.com/pytorch/extension-cpp), [CPython Stable Limited API](https://docs.python.org/3/c-api/stable.html).

original_blocks.text:
# Custom C++ and CUDA Operators — Complete Content Extraction

**Source:** PyTorch Tutorials 2.14.0+cu130 documentation
**Author:** [Richard Zou](https://github.com/zou3519)
**Dates:** Created Jun 18, 2024 | Last Updated Jan 20, 2026 | Last Verified Nov 05, 2024

---

## Overview

**What you will learn:**
- How to integrate custom operators written in C++/CUDA with PyTorch
- How to test custom operators using `torch.library.opcheck`

**Prerequisites:**
- **PyTorch 2.4 or later** (or **PyTorch 2.10 or later if using the stable ABI**)
- Basic understanding of C++ and CUDA programming

> **Note:** This tutorial will also work on AMD ROCm with no additional modifications.

PyTorch offers a large library of operators that work on Tensors (e.g. `torch.add`, `torch.sum`, etc). This tutorial demonstrates the blessed path to authoring a custom operator written in C++/CUDA.

The tutorial authors a **fused multiply-add** C++ and CUDA operator with these semantics:

```python
def mymuladd(a: Tensor, b: Tensor, c: float):
    return a * b + c
```

The end-to-end working example is in the [extension-cpp](https://github.com/pytorch/extension-cpp) repository, containing two parallel implementations:
- [extension_cpp/](https://github.com/pytorch/extension-cpp/tree/master/extension_cpp): Uses the standard ATen/LibTorch API.
- [extension_cpp_stable/](https://github.com/pytorch/extension-cpp/tree/master/extension_cpp_stable): Uses APIs supported by the LibTorch Stable ABI (recommended for PyTorch 2.10+).

**Which API should you use?**
- **ABI-Stable LibTorch API** (recommended): For PyTorch 2.10+. Build a **single wheel** that works across multiple PyTorch versions (2.10, 2.11, 2.12, etc.), reducing maintenance burden.
- **Non-ABI-Stable LibTorch API**: Use if you need APIs not yet available in the stable ABI, or if targeting PyTorch versions older than 2.10. You will need to build separate wheels for each PyTorch version.

---

## 1. Setting Up the Build System

Use `torch.utils.cpp_extension` to compile custom C++/CUDA code. C++ extensions may be built "ahead of time" with setuptools, or "just in time" via [load_inline](https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/cpp_extension.html#torch.utils.cpp_extension.load_inline); the tutorial focuses on the "ahead of time" flavor.

### `setup.py` — ABI-Stable LibTorch API

```python
from setuptools import setup, Extension
from torch.utils import cpp_extension

setup(name="extension_cpp",
      ext_modules=[
          cpp_extension.CppExtension(
              "extension_cpp",
              ["muladd.cpp"],
              extra_compile_args={
                  "cxx": [
                      # define Py_LIMITED_API with min version 3.9 to expose only the stable
                      # limited API subset from Python.h
                      "-DPy_LIMITED_API=0x03090000",
                      # define TORCH_TARGET_VERSION with min version 2.10 to expose only the
                      # stable API subset from torch
                      "-DTORCH_TARGET_VERSION=0x020a000000000000",
                  ]
              },
              py_limited_api=True)],  # Build 1 wheel across multiple Python versions
      cmdclass={'build_ext': cpp_extension.BuildExtension},
      options={"bdist_wheel": {"py_limited_api": "cp39"}}  # 3.9 is minimum supported Python version
)
```

### `setup.py` — Non-ABI-Stable LibTorch API

```python
from setuptools import setup, Extension
from torch.utils import cpp_extension

setup(name="extension_cpp",
      ext_modules=[
          cpp_extension.CppExtension(
              "extension_cpp",
              ["muladd.cpp"],
              extra_compile_args={
                  "cxx": [
                      "-DPy_LIMITED_API=0x03090000",
                  ]
              },
              py_limited_api=True)],
      cmdclass={'build_ext': cpp_extension.BuildExtension},
      options={"bdist_wheel": {"py_limited_api": "cp39"}}
)
```

> If you need to compile CUDA code (for example, `.cu` files), then instead use **`torch.utils.cpp_extension.CUDAExtension`**. See [extension-cpp](https://github.com/pytorch/extension-cpp) for an example of how this is set up.

### CPython Agnosticism

The above examples represent a **CPython agnostic wheel** — a single wheel that can run across multiple CPython versions. The minimum supported version is 3.9. Build the extension in the same environment as the minimum CPython version (here, CPython 3.9).

Three key lines to note:

1. **`Py_LIMITED_API`** in `extra_compile_args`:
```python
extra_compile_args={"cxx": ["-DPy_LIMITED_API=0x03090000"]},
```
Defining `Py_LIMITED_API` verifies the extension only uses the [CPython Stable Limited API](https://docs.python.org/3/c-api/stable.html), a requirement for a CPython agnostic wheel. Avoid unstable CPython APIs (e.g., from `libtorch_python`, in particular pytorch/python bindings) and only use APIs from `libtorch` (ATen objects, operators and the dispatcher). Defining this flag is not a full guarantee but is strongly recommended.

2. & 3. **`py_limited_api`** (informs setuptools and influences wheel naming):
```python
setup(name="extension_cpp",
      ext_modules=[
          cpp_extension.CppExtension(
            ...,
            py_limited_api=True)],  # Build 1 wheel across multiple Python versions
      ...,
      options={"bdist_wheel": {"py_limited_api": "cp39"}}  # 3.9 is minimum supported Python version
)
```
Both placements are necessary (`py_limited_api=True` as an argument to `CppExtension`/`CUDAExtension`, and as an option to the `"bdist_wheel"` command with the minimal supported CPython version).

If your extension uses CPython APIs outside the stable limited set, you **cannot** build a CPython agnostic wheel — build one wheel per CPython version:

```python
from setuptools import setup, Extension
from torch.utils import cpp_extension

setup(name="extension_cpp",
      ext_modules=[
          cpp_extension.CppExtension(
            "extension_cpp",
            ["muladd.cpp"])],
      cmdclass={'build_ext': cpp_extension.BuildExtension},
)
```

### LibTorch Stable ABI (PyTorch Agnosticism)

LibTorch agnosticism allows building a single wheel that works across multiple PyTorch versions (2.10, 2.11, 2.12, etc.). CPython agnosticism and LibTorch agnosticism are orthogonal and can be combined.

To achieve LibTorch agnosticism, use the ABI stable LibTorch API — e.g., instead of `at::Tensor`, use `torch::stable::Tensor`. See the [LibTorch Stable ABI documentation](https://pytorch.org/docs/main/notes/libtorch_stable_abi.html).

The stable ABI `setup.py` includes `TORCH_TARGET_VERSION=0x020a000000000000`. **Version format: `[MAJ 1 byte][MIN 1 byte][PATCH 1 byte][ABI TAG 5 bytes]`, so 2.10.0 = `0x020a000000000000`.**

---

## 2. Defining the Custom Op and Adding Backend Implementations

### CPU implementation — ABI-Stable LibTorch API

```cpp
#include <torch/csrc/stable/library.h>
#include <torch/csrc/stable/ops.h>
#include <torch/csrc/stable/tensor.h>
#include <torch/headeronly/core/ScalarType.h>
#include <torch/headeronly/macros/Macros.h>

torch::stable::Tensor mymuladd_cpu(
    const torch::stable::Tensor& a,
    const torch::stable::Tensor& b,
    double c) {
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.sizes().equals(b.sizes()));
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.scalar_type() == torch::headeronly::ScalarType::Float);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(b.scalar_type() == torch::headeronly::ScalarType::Float);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.device().type() == torch::headeronly::DeviceType::CPU);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(b.device().type() == torch::headeronly::DeviceType::CPU);

  torch::stable::Tensor a_contig = torch::stable::contiguous(a);
  torch::stable::Tensor b_contig = torch::stable::contiguous(b);
  torch::stable::Tensor result = torch::stable::empty_like(a_contig);

  const float* a_ptr = a_contig.const_data_ptr<float>();
  const float* b_ptr = b_contig.const_data_ptr<float>();
  float* result_ptr = result.mutable_data_ptr<float>();

  for (int64_t i = 0; i < result.numel(); i++) {
    result_ptr[i] = a_ptr[i] * b_ptr[i] + c;
  }
  return result;
}
```

### CPU implementation — Non-ABI-Stable LibTorch API

```cpp
#include <ATen/Operators.h>
#include <torch/all.h>
#include <torch/library.h>

at::Tensor mymuladd_cpu(const at::Tensor& a, const at::Tensor& b, double c) {
  TORCH_CHECK(a.sizes() == b.sizes());
  TORCH_CHECK(a.dtype() == at::kFloat);
  TORCH_CHECK(b.dtype() == at::kFloat);
  TORCH_INTERNAL_ASSERT(a.device().type() == at::DeviceType::CPU);
  TORCH_INTERNAL_ASSERT(b.device().type() == at::DeviceType::CPU);
  at::Tensor a_contig = a.contiguous();
  at::Tensor b_contig = b.contiguous();
  at::Tensor result = torch::empty(a_contig.sizes(), a_contig.options());
  const float* a_ptr = a_contig.data_ptr<float>();
  const float* b_ptr = b_contig.data_ptr<float>();
  float* result_ptr = result.data_ptr<float>();
  for (int64_t i = 0; i < result.numel(); i++) {
    result_ptr[i] = a_ptr[i] * b_ptr[i] + c;
  }
  return result;
}
```

To use this from PyTorch's Python frontend, register it as a PyTorch operator using the `TORCH_LIBRARY` (or `STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY`) macro. This automatically binds the operator to Python.

**Operator registration is a two-step process:**
1. **Defining the operator** — ensures PyTorch is aware of the new operator.
2. **Registering backend implementations** — associates implementations for various backends (CPU, CUDA) with the operator.

### Defining an Operator

Steps:
1. Select a namespace (recommended: your top-level project name; here "extension_cpp").
2. Provide a schema string specifying input/output types and whether any input Tensor will be mutated. (More types than Tensor and float are supported — see [The Custom Operators Manual](https://pytorch.org/docs/main/notes/custom_operators.html).)
   - If authoring an operator that can mutate its input Tensors, see the "Creating mutable operators" section.

**ABI-Stable LibTorch API:**
```cpp
STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY(extension_cpp, m) {
  // Note that "float" in the schema corresponds to the C++ double type
  // and the Python float type.
  m.def("mymuladd(Tensor a, Tensor b, float c) -> Tensor");
}
```

**Non-ABI-Stable LibTorch API:**
```cpp
TORCH_LIBRARY(extension_cpp, m) {
  // Note that "float" in the schema corresponds to the C++ double type
  // and the Python float type.
  m.def("mymuladd(Tensor a, Tensor b, float c) -> Tensor");
}
```

This makes the operator available from Python via `torch.ops.extension_cpp.mymuladd`.

### Registering Backend Implementations

Use `TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL` (or `STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL`) to register a backend implementation.

**ABI-Stable LibTorch API** — note the function pointer is wrapped with `TORCH_BOX()` (required for stable ABI functions to handle argument boxing/unboxing correctly):
```cpp
STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL(extension_cpp, CPU, m) {
  m.impl("mymuladd", TORCH_BOX(&mymuladd_cpu));
}
```

**Non-ABI-Stable LibTorch API:**
```cpp
TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL(extension_cpp, CPU, m) {
  m.impl("mymuladd", &mymuladd_cpu);
}
```

### CUDA Kernel + Registration — ABI-Stable LibTorch API

```cpp
#include <torch/csrc/stable/library.h>
#include <torch/csrc/stable/ops.h>
#include <torch/csrc/stable/tensor.h>
#include <torch/csrc/stable/c/shim.h>
#include <cuda.h>
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__global__ void muladd_kernel(int numel, const float* a, const float* b, float c, float* result) {
  int idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
  if (idx < numel) result[idx] = a[idx] * b[idx] + c;
}

torch::stable::Tensor mymuladd_cuda(
    const torch::stable::Tensor& a,
    const torch::stable::Tensor& b,
    double c) {
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.sizes().equals(b.sizes()));
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.scalar_type() == torch::headeronly::ScalarType::Float);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(b.scalar_type() == torch::headeronly::ScalarType::Float);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(a.device().type() == torch::headeronly::DeviceType::CUDA);
  STD_TORCH_CHECK(b.device().type() == torch::headeronly::DeviceType::CUDA);

  torch::stable::Tensor a_contig = torch::stable::contiguous(a);
  torch::stable::Tensor b_contig = torch::stable::contiguous(b);
  torch::stable::Tensor result = torch::stable::empty_like(a_contig);

  const float* a_ptr = a_contig.const_data_ptr<float>();
  const float* b_ptr = b_contig.const_data_ptr<float>();
  float* result_ptr = result.mutable_data_ptr<float>();

  int numel = a_contig.numel();

  // For now, we rely on the raw shim API to get the current CUDA stream.
  // This will be improved in a future release.
  // When using a raw shim API, we need to use TORCH_ERROR_CODE_CHECK to
  // check the error code and throw an appropriate runtime_error otherwise.
  void* stream_ptr = nullptr;
  TORCH_ERROR_CODE_CHECK(
      aoti_torch_get_current_cuda_stream(a.get_device_index(), &stream_ptr));
  cudaStream_t stream = static_cast<cudaStream_t>(stream_ptr);

  muladd_kernel<<<(numel+255)/256, 256, 0, stream>>>(numel, a_ptr, b_ptr, c, result_ptr);
  return result;
}

STABLE_TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL(extension_cpp, CUDA, m) {
  m.impl("mymuladd", TORCH_BOX(&mymuladd_cuda));
}
```

### CUDA Kernel + Registration — Non-ABI-Stable LibTorch API

```cpp
#include <ATen/Operators.h>
#include <torch/all.h>
#include <torch/library.h>
#include <cuda.h>
#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <ATen/cuda/CUDAContext.h>

__global__ void muladd_kernel(int numel, const float* a, const float* b, float c, float* result) {
  int idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
  if (idx < numel) result[idx] = a[idx] * b[idx] + c;
}

at::Tensor mymuladd_cuda(const at::Tensor& a, const at::Tensor& b, double c) {
  TORCH_CHECK(a.sizes() == b.sizes());
  TORCH_CHECK(a.dtype() == at::kFloat);
  TORCH_CHECK(b.dtype() == at::kFloat);
  TORCH_INTERNAL_ASSERT(a.device().type() == at::DeviceType::CUDA);
  TORCH_INTERNAL_ASSERT(b.device().type() == at::DeviceType::CUDA);
  at::Tensor a_contig = a.contiguous();
  at::Tensor b_contig = b.contiguous();
  at::Tensor result = at::empty(a_contig.sizes(), a_contig.options());
  const float* a_ptr = a_contig.data_ptr<float>();
  const float* b_ptr = b_contig.data_ptr<float>();
  float* result_ptr = result.data_ptr<float>();

  int numel = a_contig.numel();
  cudaStream_t stream = at::cuda::getCurrentCUDAStream();
  muladd_kernel<<<(numel+255)/256, 256, 0, stream>>>(numel, a_ptr, b_ptr, c, result_ptr);
  return result;
}

TORCH_LIBRARY_IMPL(extension_cpp, CUDA, m) {
  m.impl("mymuladd", &mymuladd_cuda);
}
```

